    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="A denser fluid layer sits atop a lighter one -- exactly the unstable arrangement -- separated by an interface with a small sinusoidal ripple. The two layers are represented not as a sharp density jump but through the Boussinesq approximation: a dimensionless buoyancy field b=-\delta\rho/\rho_0 (positive where the fluid is locally lighter than the reference density), advected and diffused like vorticity but also exerting a baroclinic torque g\,\partial_x b on it wherever the density gradient is non-vertical:">

